					Online Compass Geometry & Math: The Mathematics of the 360° Circle
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
					Why does a compass have 360 degrees, and how does compass geometry connect to trigonometry, angle construction, and cartography? Whether you are a student exploring <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">online compass math</strong>, a surveyor calculating azimuth angles, or someone seeking an <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">online compass for geometry</strong>, understanding circle geometry unlocks the mathematical foundation of navigation.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">1. The 360-Degree Circle Geometry</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							In Euclidean plane geometry, a full rotation forms a circle of exactly <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">360 degrees</strong>. This sexagesimal division dates back to ancient Babylonian astronomers, chosen because 360 has 24 divisors (including 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, 18, 20, 24, 30, 36, 40, 45, 60, 72, 90, 120, and 180). This makes dividing a <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">360 degree compass online</strong> into quadrants, octants, and half-quadrants mathematically clean without recurring fractions.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">2. Compass Degrees Online vs. Radians</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							While field navigators read <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">compass degrees online</strong> (0° to 360°), computer graphics and physics engines calculate bearings in <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">radians</strong>. The geometric conversion between degrees and radians is governed by the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter:
						</p>
						<div class="my-4 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-4 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink">
							<p>θ_radians = θ_degrees × (π / 180°)</p>
							<p class="mt-1">θ_degrees = θ_radians × (180° / π)</p>
							<p class="mt-2 text-mute text-xs">// 0° (North) = 0 rad | 90° (East) = π/2 rad ≈ 1.5708 rad | 180° (South) = π rad ≈ 3.1416 rad | 270° (West) = 3π/2 rad ≈ 4.7124 rad</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">3. Geometric Drawing Compass vs. Navigational Compass Tool</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
							In academic geometry, an <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">online compass tool geometry</strong> usually refers to a geometric pair of compasses used for compass-and-straightedge constructions—drawing circles, arcs, bisecting line segments, and constructing perpendicular angles. In contrast, our navigational <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">online digital compass</strong> measures orientation relative to the physical Earth. If you need a clean digital canvas to sketch geometric figures, plot angle diagrams, or draw navigation bearings by hand, you can use <a href="https://simplesketchpad.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-link underline hover:text-ink">Simple Sketchpad</a> directly in your browser without installing any software. The table below highlights how these two tools complement each other:
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Comparison Table: Geometry Compass vs Navigational Digital Compass -->
